PCA (Principal Component Analysis) is a way to reduce (Combine) Features into Combined Features which reduces the dimensionality 
of the features available
Ex:(Strength Speed Stamina Balance Cars,Land owned) can be turned into two features (Physical Attributes,Wealth),for example
You can say that physical attributes is a linear combination of (Strength,Speed,Stamina) and Wealth (Balance,Cars,Land owned)
To get these PCA Components we first need to standarize the data by subtracting each feature from its feature's mean and dividing by 
standard deviation.This is because the variance between data can change by units (from 1kg to 2kg) and from (2lbs to 4lbs) essentially
the same weight but different deviaton due to the unit dividing by standard deviation solves this problem.The data is centered around the
origin by default so we subtract from the data the mean so that its around the mean not the origin.
We then from the standarized data calculate the covariance matrix which describes the relation between each variable 
Now the original idea behind PCA is to find the line that fits most of the data so that the variance between each projected data on that
point is great (The greater the variance between the points means that the two features are distinguishable otherwise redundant) 
So we need to find the vector (line) that gives us maximum variance which is the first eigenvector (First PCA) of the Covariance matrix.
and the Second eigenvector is the second PCA with Second-Best maximum variance up until nth eigenvector where n is the dimension of the
Covariance matrix (nxn) 
Now that we have the PCA components we can take each sample from our dataset and project their original features into the PCA components
by doing matrix dot product (Remember that PCA components are linear combination of the original features in a sample) thus we can compute
the score of any desired sample in PCA components

